      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I understand your concerns, and it's essential to stay proactive. Legally, you have rights as a consumer, so here are steps you can take to safeguard your funds:\n\n1. Review the account agreement and any information the credit union provided about fees and account closure. Understanding these terms may help you contest any that seem unfair or inconsistent with your knowledge at the beginning.\n\n2. Contact a consumer protection agency. In the U.S., the Consumer Financial Protection Bureau (CFPB) or the National Credit Union Administration (NCUA) can be valuable resources. You can file a complaint against the credit union, and they can investigate your case.\n\n3. Consider closing the account. If you're facing an immediate threat of further fees or loss of control over your funds, moving your money to a different institution may be wise. However, consult with a legal advisor beforehand to understand the implications of such a move.\n\n4. Engage an attorney. Legal representation can help you understand your options, potentially argue your case if there's ground for litigation against the credit union, or help you in making an officially documented arduous to understand request for account closure or your withdrawal of funds.\n\n5. Bank Account UpdoOpeS: Some services can help monitor your credit union account for unusual",
